<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::political_will_backers" target="global_financial_stress::sovereign_yield_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Stronger, visible political support from backer governments reduces perceived tail‑risk of spillovers and implicit credit risk for proximate developed economies, lowering risk premia and benchmark sovereign yields.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_financial_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::political_will_backers" target="iran_houthi_escalation::maritime_incident_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising political will among Western backers to sustain Ukraine leads to diversion of limited naval assets and rules‑of‑engagement political capital toward European deterrence, reducing patrols/deterrence in the Red Sea/Arabian Sea and thereby enabling more Houthi attacks and seizures.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_houthi_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::political_will_backers" target="north_korea_nuclear::strategic_alignment" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained high political support among Ukraine backers further isolates Russia, prompting Moscow to deepen reciprocal military-diplomatic cooperation with Pyongyang (e.g., security consultations, coordinated messaging, barter deals), thereby raising perceived strategic alignment.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">north_korea_nuclear</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::political_will_backers" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_control_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Stronger domestic and allied political will to punish or isolate Russia leads governments to broaden and tighten export control regimes (and to coordinate allied controls), often extending scrutiny to dual‑use AI and semiconductor technologies relevant to US–China competition.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
