<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="global_financial_stress::corporate_spread_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">A sudden increase in export‑control intensity raises near‑term revenue and supply‑chain uncertainty for affected tech manufacturers and suppliers, prompting credit investors to reprice default and liquidity risk and demand wider corporate spreads.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_financial_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="iran_houthi_escalation::sanctions_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">A step-up in export controls tied to the US–China tech rivalry prompts allied regulatory coordination and intelligence sharing that expands targeted designations and trade/financial restrictions on intermediary firms and networks supplying Iran/Houthi proxies, raising sanctions and enforcement intensity.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_houthi_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="north_korea_nuclear::tech_diffusion_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Stricter US/China export controls directly block legal shipments and licensing of AI-relevant hardware, software and documentation to intermediaries and suppliers that would otherwise transfer dual-use capabilities to North Korean actors, reducing the speed and volume of technology diffusion.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">north_korea_nuclear</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="north_korea_nuclear::cross_border_cap_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Tightened export controls increase compliance, screening and legal risks for banks and investors, prompting intermediaries and private financiers to reduce transactions with flagged entities or jurisdictions and thereby lowering cross-border capital flows that could finance proliferation-related activities linked to North Korea.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">north_korea_nuclear</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::export_control_intensity" target="russia_ukraine_war::political_will_backers" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A marked tightening of export controls on AI and semiconductor tech signals an escalatory US‑China contest and provokes allied policy narratives about strategic competition, which increases domestic political support among backers for sustained or expanded assistance to Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
